{"kind":"request","text":"Export a typed stand-in grant from `packages/derive`, so that a replica outside the runtime can call `judgeAct` to predict an act with no type cast.\n\nFiled 2026-11-12 22:26 EST at Hugh's request, from the dap-foldkit plan (`~/play/dap-foldkit/PLAN.md`, draft 3 revision 3, change D2). Facts marked \"read\" are read at main `7ea170c9`. Facts marked \"M0\" come from `~/play/dap-foldkit/probe/RESULTS.md`, a probe that another session ran on 2026-11-12 against main `a76764a7`. I did not rerun it.\n\nWhy this matters. A browser replica predicts an act with dap's own `judgeAct`. The judge takes a `Grant`, which has an `issued` fact and a freshness proof. A browser holds neither. The plan asked whether dap needs a separate prediction entry. M0 answers no: a stand-in grant satisfies the judge, and the prediction then equals what the scope writes for the same signed bytes. The stand-in has two fabricated parts (M0): an `issued` fact, and `fresh: null as unknown as ObservationUse`. That cast is the one dap's own fixtures use (`packages/derive/test/fixtures.ts:68`, read).\n\nA cast in every client that predicts is a weak seam. A change to `Grant` or to how the judge reads `fresh` (`packages/derive/src/judge.ts:368` and `:372`, read) then breaks each client at run time and not at compile time.\n\nWhat to build. One exported function in `packages/derive`, for example `predictionGrant(definition, actor, scope)`, that returns a grant of every declared action for one key, marked current, typed with no cast at the call site. Its documentation states what a prediction with it can and cannot answer: it never answers `unauthorized`, because authority is assumed, and only the scope decides authority. The fixtures in `packages/derive/test` can use the same function.\n\nNo change to the judge's rules, to an entry, or to the wire. If the typed form needs `fresh` to be optional on a type, the request owes a statement that no runtime path can then pass a grant without a proof.\n\nSize: small. One function, its type, its tests.\n\nNeeded by: nothing in dap-foldkit waits for it. M2 uses the cast. This head removes the cast before the replica module moves into `packages/client`, which the plan proposes once a second application uses it. Lowest priority of the three dap-foldkit requests.\n\nA related finding from M0, for the same documentation: a prediction checks `notAfter` against the clock reading that the caller passes. A browser passes its own clock, so a large skew predicts `expired` wrongly. The scope's answer is not affected.","body":{"conditions":"packages/derive exports one function that returns a stand-in grant for prediction, typed with no cast at the call site. Its documentation states that a prediction with it never answers unauthorized and that the caller's clock decides expired. A test shows that a prediction with it equals the entry the judge seals for the same signed bytes. No change to the judge's rules, to an entry, or to the wire, and no runtime path can pass a grant without a freshness proof. The gate passes.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}